00:00:03.379 --> 00:00:05.660 align:start position:0%
the harpy a mythical creature in Greek  mythology with the body of a bird and  the face of a woman the harpies were  known as the hounds of Zeus as they  would often be dispatched to do his  bidding on earth they were sent as a  form of punishment stealing food from  their victims and even carrying some  away to be judged by the goddess of  retribution and vengeance their name  translated means snatches which is  reflected in their actions as they would  swoop down and snatch people away from  earth the harpies were sometimes seen as  spirits of the wind and in some Greek  poems they were not physical beings at  all rather the storm winds personified  the appearance of the harpy is one that  varies in Greek mythology in early  stories they were described as fairlock  to winged maidens with Grecian pottery  depicting harpies as beautiful women  with wings the later depictions of  harpies described them as ugly and  disgusting creatures who gave off a  putrid odor they will cruel and  terrifying of a ravenous hunger they had  sharp talons made of metal that they  would use slash and shred their victims  some believed that they were the cousins  of the Gorgons the three sisters of hair  made of venomous snakes and the gaze  that could turn anyone into stone one of  the most well-known stories involving  harpies is that of Jason and the  Argonauts and King Phineas of Thrace  Zeus gave Phineas the gift of prophecy  and in return he used it against the  gods as punishment Zeus blinded the king  and he was sentenced to live on an  island of a buffet of food that he could  never eat the harpies would swoop down  every morning and steal all of the food  before Phineas was able to satisfy his  hunger years later Phineas was rescued  from his fate by Jason and the Argonauts  as they drove the harpies away in this  story we can see the harpies or an agent  of punishment  as with many creatures in Greek  mythology they have been known to evolve  with time the harpies were once  considered desirable and majestic  creatures they later became ugly cruel  monsters who were used as an agent of  punishment against evildoers if you've  enjoyed this video then please go ahead  and leave it a like consider subscribing  if folklore mythology is something that  interests you if you have anything you'd  like to discuss or have any ideas for  future videos then please go ahead and  let me know in the comments below as  always I've been your host mythology and  folklore explained
 

